# Resistive wall wakefield on the GPU.
#
#   mpirun -n 4 genesis4 wake_gpu.in
#
# A `&wake` block is supported on the GPU. The loss profile is one number per
# slice, because a wake is driven by the slice current rather than by individual
# particles, so the host builds the profile exactly as it does on the CPU path
# and the GPU only adds it to the particles.
#
# To check it against the CPU, copy this file, set gpu=false, and compare. To
# see how much the wake is doing, delete the &wake block and compare against
# that instead: on this deck it shifts Beam/energy by about 4e-5 and
# Beam/bunching by about 8e-3, which is far above the CPU-to-GPU difference.
#
# Setting transient=true makes the host recompute the convolution on every step
# rather than once. That is host work, identical on both paths, and it costs
# roughly 2.6x the runtime here.

&setup
rootname=wake_gpu
lattice=Aramis.lat
beamline=ARAMIS
lambda0=1e-10
gamma0=11357.82
delz=0.045000
shotnoise=1
seed=1001
nbins=8
beam_global_stat=true
field_global_stat=true
&end

&lattice
zmatch=9.5
&end

&time
slen=4.0e-7
sample=8
&end

&field
power=0
dgrid=2.000000e-04
ngrid=256
waist_size=30e-6
&end

&beam
current=3000
delgam=1.000000
ex=4.000000e-07
ey=4.000000e-07
&end

&wake
material=CU
radius=2.5e-3
roundpipe=true
transient=false
&end

&track
gpu=true
fft_fieldsolver=true
zstop=10
&end
